\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Discussion}
\title{A sum of decaying exponentials}
\date{2026-11-06}

\p{A finite graph has finitely many nonnegative Laplacian
eigenvalues, and a connected graph has #{0} once.
Form #{Z(t)} by summing #{e^{-t\lambda}} over that list. The
quantity #{-2\,d\log Z/d\log t} still depends on the positive
time #{t}. On one graph, can this quantity stay near a
single constant on intervals of times whose endpoint
quotient grows without a bound?}
